<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The mcremote daemon now terminates TLS (self-signed cert, generated on first
  run) and the app pins its SHA-256 fingerprint from the pair QR. Cleartext is
  therefore off app-wide: a ws:// connection would carry the device token in
  the clear across the mesh.

  NOTE ON TRUST ANCHORS: there is deliberately no <trust-anchors> entry for the
  daemon's certificate here. Android's network security config can only pin a
  CA that is known at build time, and every user's daemon mints its own cert at
  runtime — there is nothing to bundle. Verification happens in Dart instead
  (CertPinner in lib/data/ws/mcremote_client.dart), which builds an HttpClient
  with withTrustedRoots:false and accepts exactly the one leaf whose SHA-256
  matches the fingerprint scanned during pairing. That is strictly stronger
  than trusting a CA: it admits one certificate, not any certificate that CA
  chooses to issue.

  The system trust anchors below still apply to everything else the app might
  talk to (they are the platform default; listed explicitly so that adding a
  <domain-config> later cannot silently widen them).

  If you must run a plaintext daemon (`mcremote serve --tls=false`), do NOT
  re-enable cleartext globally. Add a narrow <domain-config> for that one host:

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">100.64.0.1</domain>
    </domain-config>
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system"/>
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
